1
00:00:00,135 --> 00:00:02,933
♪ [music] ♪

2
00:00:12,065 --> 00:00:14,476
- [Narrator] The famous comedian
Groucho Marx once said,

3
00:00:14,476 --> 00:00:16,145
- [Groucho] "I don't want 
to belong to any club

4
00:00:16,145 --> 00:00:18,046
that would accept me as a member."

5
00:00:18,046 --> 00:00:20,667
- [Narrator] Believe it or not, 
the economist George Akerlof

6
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:22,668
won a Nobel Prize for analyzing

7
00:00:22,668 --> 00:00:24,891
when Groucho-type
reasoning makes sense

8
00:00:24,892 --> 00:00:26,507
and what the consequences are.

9
00:00:26,507 --> 00:00:27,605
Groucho was using the fact

10
00:00:27,605 --> 00:00:29,638
that the club was offering
him membership

11
00:00:29,638 --> 00:00:31,766
to infer something about
the quality of the club.

12
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:34,446
- "Yeah, because it's
not very exclusive."

13
00:00:34,446 --> 00:00:36,766
- Akerlof analyzed
the more general situation

14
00:00:36,766 --> 00:00:38,695
of adverse selection

15
00:00:38,695 --> 00:00:41,266
when an offer conveys
negative information

16
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:43,085
about what is being offered.

17
00:00:43,085 --> 00:00:46,457
Akerlof's famous example 
was the market for used cars.

18
00:00:46,457 --> 00:00:49,637
Suppose that used cars 
come in a variety of qualities.

19
00:00:49,637 --> 00:00:51,057
>From the worst -- the lemons,

20
00:00:51,057 --> 00:00:52,926
the cars that always
are breaking down,

21
00:00:52,926 --> 00:00:56,816
to the very best, 
the most reliable cars -- the plums.

22
00:00:56,816 --> 00:00:59,026
The sellers know 
the quality of their car,

23
00:00:59,026 --> 00:01:01,097
but suppose
that the buyers can't tell

24
00:01:01,097 --> 00:01:03,916
which used cars are lemons 
and which are plums.

25
00:01:03,916 --> 00:01:06,498
Since the sellers have
more information than the buyers,

26
00:01:06,498 --> 00:01:09,575
this is a model
of asymmetric information.

27
00:01:09,575 --> 00:01:11,257
Since the buyers 
can't tell the difference

28
00:01:11,257 --> 00:01:12,874
between a lemon and a plum,

29
00:01:12,874 --> 00:01:14,456
they won't be willing to pay more

30
00:01:14,456 --> 00:01:17,178
than what an average
quality car is worth.

31
00:01:17,178 --> 00:01:19,356
But seeing that the buyers 
are only willing to pay

32
00:01:19,356 --> 00:01:20,457
for average quality,

33
00:01:20,457 --> 00:01:22,647
sellers of the highest quality cars --

34
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:23,687
the plums --

35
00:01:23,687 --> 00:01:25,156
will exit the market.

36
00:01:25,156 --> 00:01:27,698
When the highest quality cars 
exit the market, however,

37
00:01:27,698 --> 00:01:30,015
the average quality of car falls,

38
00:01:30,015 --> 00:01:33,886
which reduces the price the buyers 
are willing to pay even more.

39
00:01:33,886 --> 00:01:35,242
And that causes the sellers

40
00:01:35,242 --> 00:01:37,325
of the next
highest quality used cars

41
00:01:37,325 --> 00:01:39,475
to drop out of the market as well.

42
00:01:39,475 --> 00:01:42,326
At the end of what is sometimes 
is called "the death spiral,"

43
00:01:42,326 --> 00:01:44,634
the market collapses
and buyers conclude,

44
00:01:44,635 --> 00:01:45,787
just like Groucho,

45
00:01:45,787 --> 00:01:46,726
that they wouldn't want

46
00:01:46,726 --> 00:01:49,546
to buy any car
that is offered for sale.

47
00:01:49,546 --> 00:01:52,476
Of course, in the real world 
the used car market is thriving.

48
00:01:52,476 --> 00:01:55,798
Some 40 million used cars 
are sold every year --

49
00:01:55,798 --> 00:01:58,838
more than three times 
the number of new cars.

50
00:01:58,838 --> 00:02:01,027
That doesn't mean
the model is wrong.

51
00:02:01,027 --> 00:02:04,057
It means that over time 
the market has developed solutions

52
00:02:04,057 --> 00:02:06,428
to the asymmetric
information problem.

53
00:02:06,428 --> 00:02:09,437
Solutions like inspections,
CARFAX reports,

54
00:02:09,437 --> 00:02:11,407
and certified pre-owned programs

55
00:02:11,407 --> 00:02:14,170
that offer buyers
guarantees of quality.

56
00:02:14,170 --> 00:02:16,718
The Adverse Selection model 
also has implications

57
00:02:16,718 --> 00:02:18,608
far beyond the used car market,

58
00:02:18,608 --> 00:02:20,910
most importantly
to understanding debates

59
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:22,097
over health insurance,

60
00:02:22,097 --> 00:02:24,508
as we discuss in future videos.

61
00:02:25,638 --> 00:02:27,072
